# General Conference
Tamil title: பொது மாநாடு

The following is a summary of the valedictory address delivered by Arignar Anna at the conference on 16 July 1961.

Our General Conference, which has been conducted splendidly over these four days, has now reached its concluding stage.

Because of the devastation caused by the floods, comrades from that district could not participate in the conference as expected. Nevertheless, the conference has been successful on a scale of which all of us may be proud.

I was born into a very ordinary family. You did not make me your leader because you trusted in the distinction of my family or the power of its wealth. I have gained this strength only through your strength. Hundreds of thousands of younger brothers and sisters have entrusted their hearts to me. I cannot be certain whether I possess the physical strength to match the trust and respect you show me; but there has been no lack of strength in my enthusiasm.

I am indeed a very ordinary man. But the task I have undertaken is immense: the task of redeeming this country. We are trying to win through goodwill a victory that might otherwise be won only with bombs.

I wish to tell my respected comrades in the opposing parties, those who are indifferent to us, and the press: anyone who looks at this conference can see that the DMK is growing faster with every passing moment. Propaganda was spread throughout the country that the DMK had become unsettled merely because half a dozen speakers had left it. During these four days, however, in Tirupparankundram and in every street of Madurai, we have seen the Kazhagam's growth in the radiance of people's eyes. Let at least this make the opposing parties and humane journalists recognise the strength of the Kazhagam.

There appear to be more people at this conference from rural areas than from the city. We see people who have brought bundles of clothes, tin trunks and palm-leaf boxes along with their companions; people who have arrived in bullock carts; mothers who have tied their unadorned hair into knots; and parents who have slung cradles and put their children to sleep. From this we can understand that the DMK is gaining influence and growing even more strongly in the villages than in the cities.

Another truth has emerged through this conference. We heard comrades from Andhra, Kerala and Karnataka compete with the Tamil speakers, speaking in their respective languages with a style and feeling even more rousing than the Tamil speeches. Tamils listened to the gracious words of the Karnataka comrades, the wise counsel of the Andhra comrades and the dignified speeches of the Malayalam comrades.

A comrade from Saudi Arabia and several comrades from the Chittagong region wrote to me that they would attend, and they have come. Five hundred people have arrived to invite me to inaugurate the Kazhagam in Hyderabad. Delegates and speakers from throughout Dravida Nadu have come and participated.

The DMK is growing in the manner in which it ought to grow, at the time when it ought to grow and on the scale on which it ought to grow.

The presence of the distinguished elder Mr Medappa on our platform gives us all pride and renewed confidence.

You all call me Anna, elder brother; until now, I had no one whom I could call Anna. Today I have found an elder brother. That I have found him in Karnataka makes the news all the more joyful.

Mr Medappa said, "I am not accustomed to arguing cases; I am accustomed to delivering judgments." He may have said so out of modesty before this assembly, but he became a judge only after training in the art of advocacy.

Now that he is with us, I trust he will help us, who have established a foothold in Karnataka, to march forward with courage.

I hope the opposing parties, the rulers and the press will understand one more truth from this conference. Nearly a hundred people have spoken over these four days. Can anyone say there was even the slightest obscenity in all their speeches? Can anyone claim there was communal hatred, barbarity or objectionable conduct? Did they oppose others in an obscene manner? Were any degrading words used? I do not ask them to answer me; I ask them to answer their own consciences.

Foreign leaders and reporters ask me the same sort of questions they asked our Mayor, Munusamy. A few days ago, someone from the office of *The Hindu* in Madras telephoned me. The voice sounded familiar, so I asked who it was. "It is I, Narasimhan," came the reply.

Narasimhan is an old friend who studied in my class at college. Only after all these years did I learn that he worked for *The Hindu*. I reflected on my misfortune in having been unable to obtain publicity for so long. I am not accustomed to conducting politics with a newspaper reporter constantly at my side, as is done today.

My friend Narasimhan told me that a correspondent from a major German newspaper had arrived and wished to meet me. The German correspondent sought me out to learn about the DMK and spoke with me for three hours. After all that conversation he asked, "If a large number of people support the division of the country, what evidence is there of it?" Many people ask this, and naturally they will. Can we answer them merely by saying that tens of thousands gather even if we hold a meeting at midnight or on a mountaintop?

He said, "If you wish to demonstrate that the people support partition, show it through the ballot. Raise your strength in the Legislature to fifty or sixty members and demonstrate that you can capture power. Only then will the world take notice of you."

All my professional kinship lies with journalism. There is neither quarrel nor anger between me and other journalists. If anything arises, it can only be the fever of rivalry between partners. Therefore, speaking as a journalist myself, I say that domestic and foreign correspondents will come running only when we capture the Legislature.

The enthusiasm you show for the Kazhagam must express itself by securing electoral victory. The sign of good soil is that it yields a good harvest; the sign of a good lamp is that it gives good light.

If you fail to work properly in the election, I shall say that you made me trust you and then cut my throat. Others must not look upon our ideal, which is dearer than life, and speak of it with contempt. Just as you secured victory in the Municipal Corporation, you must secure victory in the coming general election.

There is another truth to understand from this conference. It began with a ceremony paying heroic homage to departed warriors. It concludes with a ceremony presenting shields of valour to two veteran comrades of the Kazhagam.

We see that a person with moderate wealth may remain good and honest, while one who possesses excessive wealth engages in evil acts and speaks with reckless arrogance. What use is it for a country to be large if, like an excessively wealthy man, it is merely a mass of flesh? Just as the great Holy Roman Empire fell, just as the Ottoman Empire fell, and just as the British Empire is declining, every imperial power will ultimately be reduced to dust.

They mock us by asking what Annadurai, Nedunchezhiyan, Mathiazhagan and Karunanidhi can do when they possess an army, police and prisons maintained at a cost of crores.

When I enter the Legislature in 1962, if you send with me enough of our comrades to make me happy, I too can take them on. We can confront those who conceal our history, deny geographical truths and dismiss our literary evidence.

If you do not do this and merely tell me, "Anna, do what you must," what can I do? Am I a magician who can make paniyaram without flour?

A marriage cannot be completed without the sacred wedding thread. Yet it becomes the wedding thread only when tied at the proper time and in the proper manner. Because the thread strung through the *thali* is number-forty yarn, not every length of number-forty yarn becomes a wedding thread. The yarn still on the loom cannot be called a wedding thread, nor can it acquire the distinction of one.

They now say that they intend to nationalise a few things. If that is sufficient for you, vote for Congress.

The basic needs of land for cultivators, facilities for workers, ample food for the people, clothes to preserve their dignity and homes in which to live cannot be fulfilled unless capitalism is overthrown.

Whether it is the fertiliser industry, foreigners invest capital and become partners. Whether it is telephones or a motor factory, American capitalists become partners.

Foreigners are thus admitted as partners in every industry. We must not forget that it was first a trading company that enslaved India and presented it to the British Crown. The grip upon our economy now lies in foreign hands. Our own people remain mere clerks.

The TVS company operated buses for those attending this conference. It did not leave anyone waiting for transport. The company sent all its senior officials to supervise the service, and they even performed duties that the police should have carried out.

The Madras Government also operates buses. In the Government buses there is a deafening noise. If someone travels continuously in them for six months, he will indeed become deaf. When asked why, they say that a Government bus is simply like that. Who will trust a Government whose administration is so inefficient?

Does such administration not give Acharya an opportunity to criticise it? I invoke Acharya as an example only on this one matter.

I take from each source only what I need. What we need from the peacock is its plumage; what we expect from the cuckoo is its sweet voice; what we expect from the cow is milk. If the cow produces dung, those who need it may take it. Likewise, I cited only Acharya's statement on this issue and went no further.

India has never before borne a debt burden like the one it now carries. Foreign debt has grown beyond measure. What became of the sterling reserves left behind by the British? Those too have vanished, and a debt of five thousand crore rupees has arisen. If this debt burden grows further, our backs will break; we shall have to repay one thousand crore rupees in borrowed money each year.

The Congress people say, "When Annadurai speaks of debt, does he imagine we took that money for ourselves? We spent it on the country!" Menderes spoke in the same way in Turkey. I tell them: if you have the courage, examine this money. Do we have any means of repaying the debt we have incurred?

The competence of this Government can be understood from the sugar trade. The production cost of a ton of sugar in India is seven hundred rupees. Would it not have to be sold abroad for a thousand rupees to make a profit? Instead, they sell it abroad for four hundred rupees, losing three hundred rupees on each ton. With what will people who conduct business at such a loss repay the debt?

Unemployment is increasing throughout the country, and inflation has arisen. On one side there is unemployment; on the other, work is not being carried out in certain departments.

The accusations made by Gorwala, Deshmukh and Appleby, and the shortcomings revealed in audit reports, have still not been addressed.

Under Congress rule, the country's economic system is being shaped with an even stronger capitalist orientation than it had under British rule.

Foreign penetration of the economy is increasing to a dangerous extent. Government-run industries stand as symbols of administrative incompetence.

Therefore, the DMK has resolved to remove the Congress party from power. To that end, the DMK, the ally of crores of working people within the Kazhagam, is bringing together all democratic forces opposed to Congress.

The present Indian constitutional structure was created in the name of assumptions that are false and contrary to reality. All authority is concentrated in Delhi. State Governments have been reduced to institutions that merely receive financial aid.

Prime Minister Nehru, a democrat and a nationalist, is the one who can understand the true feelings of the people of the South. We therefore wish this vital problem to be resolved by him and during his own lifetime. Let us labour tirelessly for our liberation so that our ideal may be realised and fulfilled. Greetings.

---NOTES---
The source is a printed summary rather than a verbatim transcript. Its Tamil OCR is retained unchanged in the bilingual document.

The footnote after the opening page appears to identify the four conference dates as 13, 14, 15 and 16 July 1961.

The OCR contains several visibly damaged forms, including “தி. மு. ௪.”, “வளரவேண்டி௰”, “வீரரடைபோட”, “அண்டுக்கு”, “ஐஒனநாயகவாதியும்”, and a missing word in the sentence recording the DMK resolution. The translation follows clear context where possible without silently modifying the Tamil source.

The name OCRed as “ஆபில்பீ” is read as Paul H. Appleby in context, alongside A. D. Gorwala and C. D. Deshmukh, but the Tamil source remains verbatim.
